#---
# purpose: "a closure created outside a recursion and threaded back through it compiles and runs on all three paths"
#---
# The recursion here is invisible to the static call graph. `step` reaches the
# lambda only through its own parameter `k`, so no edge from `step` to the
# lambda exists in any body; the only static edge runs the other way, from the
# lambda to `step`. Caller and callee therefore sit in DIFFERENT strong
# components while their transport-shape products form a genuine cycle
# (fz-kdt.93): `ExecutableReturn(step)` names `ExecutableReturn(lambda)`
# through the grounded closure call, and `ExecutableReturn(lambda)` names
# `ExecutableReturn(step)` through the direct one.
#
# Self-application (`k.(k, ...)`) is the smallest program with that shape;
# caller-supplied continuations and threaded callbacks have it too.

fn step(k, n) do
  if n == 0 do
    0
  else
    k.(k, n - 1)
  end
end

fn main() do
  dbg(step(fn(kk, m) -> step(kk, m) end, 5))
end
